Return Keyword:
Return keyword is used to return a value from a function. It can also be used to exit a function early.

#include <iostream>

void square(double length);

int main() {
    double length;
    double area = square(length);
    double volume = cube(length);
    std::cout << "Area: " << area << "cm^2\n";
    std::cout << "Volume: " << volume << "cm^3\n";

    return 0;
}
double square(double length) {
    double result = length * length;
    return result; // returns the value of the result to the caller
}
double cube(double length) {
    double result = length * length * length;
    return result;
}

What this code does: This code defines two functions, that calculate the area and volume of the square and cube of a given length
The main function calls these functions and prints the results to the console.
Wherever you invoke the function, it will return the value of the reult to the caller.





Variable Scope:
Local variables = declares inside a function or block {}
Global Variables = declared outside of all functions

int main() {

    int myNum = 1;
    
    std::cout << myNum;
    
    return 0;
}
void printNum(){
    std::cout << myNum; // this will give an error because the myNum variable is a local variable and is not accessible outside of the main function.
}
void printNum(){
    int myNum = 2; // this is a local variable and is only accessible within the printNum function.
    std::cout << myNum; // this will print 2
}

Global variables are accesible from any function in the program, and are usually at the top of your program.
Local variables are used before any global variables.

