Memory Addresses:
A location in memory where data is stored.
A memory address can be accessed with & (address-of operator)

int main() {
    
    std::string name = "Bro";
    int age = 21;
    bool student = true;
    
    std::cout << &name << '\n'; // prints the memory address of the name variable
    std::cout << &age << '\n'; // prints the memory address of the age variable
    std::cout << &student << '\n'; // prints the memory address of the student variable
    
    return 0;
    }

This is useful for finding out how much memory is being used by a variable and for debugging purposes.

